.TH CWKEYER 1 "2026" "ham-cwkeyer" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cwkeyer \- low-latency iambic CW sidetone keyer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cwkeyer
.br
.B cwkeyer
.I /dev/input/eventX
.RI [ wpm ]
.RI [ freq_hz ]
.RI [ dit_key ]
.RI [ dah_key ]
.RI [ nograb ]
.RI [ decode ]
.RI [ straight ]
.RI [ mix\-mic ]
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-setup
.RI [ wpm ]
.RI [ freq_hz ]
.RI [ dit_key ]
.RI [ dah_key ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-help
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-version
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-list\-devices
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cwkeyer
reads a keyboard-emulating paddle adapter and generates a sidetone. It
implements the Curtis 8044 type B (Accu-keyer) iambic keyer,
with dot/dash paddle memory, and can optionally decode the keyed text. A
.B straight
mode passes the paddles through as a manual key instead.
.PP
The details of reading the paddles and playing the tone belong to the
platform: on Linux the adapter is read through evdev and the sidetone goes to
ALSA, on macOS it is read through IOKit HID and played through CoreAudio (see
.BR MACOS ).
.PP
The paddle inputs are filtered with a digital approximation of the 8044's
external RC network. The recommended 1 M\(*W/0.01 \(*mF network has a time
constant of about 10 ms.
.SH MACOS
The same binary runs on macOS, where a paddle adapter is a plain USB HID
keyboard and no driver or kernel extension is involved. The device argument
is then
.I vendor:product:location
(for example
.IR 04d8:01a6:00110000 )
as printed by
.BR \-\-list\-devices .
The last field is the USB port, so it changes when the adapter is moved; the
argument may stop at a field boundary, and
.I 04d8:01a6
selects the adapter in any port as long as that is unambiguous. The built-in
keyboard reports zeros for vendor and product, so it needs the full id, and it
is usable as a paddle with
.B nograb
and your own key names.
.PP
Reading keyboard input needs the Input Monitoring permission, which macOS
attributes to the terminal that launched the keyer. Taking exclusive control
stands in for evdev's grab and is refused for keyboards unless the process is
root, so the setup wizard offers
.B nograb
by default and the keyer warns and falls back if a seize fails. Without it the
levers also reach the foreground application. The
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
and
.B PULSE_SINK
variables do not apply; use
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
to choose the output by name or UID. Latency is the output device's buffer,
and the achieved value is printed at startup and at exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I /dev/input/eventX
The evdev device to read (Linux; see
.BR MACOS ). Use
.BR evtest (8)
or
.BR libinput (1)
to find it, or run
.B cwkeyer \-\-list\-devices
for a quick list of readable input devices. A normal keyboard is usually
.IR /dev/input/event3 .
.TP
.I wpm
Keying speed in words per minute (default 12, range 1\(en40).
At 40 WPM, the full \(+-25 ms weighting range still leaves positive
marks and spaces.
.TP
.I freq_hz
Sidetone frequency in hertz (default 800).
.TP
.IR dit_key ", " dah_key
evdev key names or codes for the dit and dah paddles (default
.B LEFTCTRL
and
.BR RIGHTCTRL ).
Known names include
.BR COMMA ,
.BR DOT ,
.BR LEFT ,
.BR RIGHT ,
.BR Z ,
and
.BR X ;
a
.B KEY_
prefix and raw numeric codes are also accepted.
.TP
.B nograb
Do not take exclusive control of the input device; useful when testing on
the keyboard you are typing on.
.TP
.B decode
Print each keyed symbol decoded to text, without newlines. Unknown symbols
are ignored, and a pause longer than seven dits prints a space.
.TP
.B straight
Use the paddles as a straight key: either lever (or both, squeezed) keys
the sidetone directly, with no iambic timing or memory. The RC paddle
filter still applies. This is the software equivalent of the 8044ABM's
separate
.B MAN. KEY
input. The
.B decode
option is ignored in this mode.
.TP
.B mix\-mic
Mix the microphone into the sidetone, so that one output device carries both
and a voice application can take the operator and the sidetone from a single
input. macOS only; use the
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
and
.B CW_MIX_MIC_DEVICE
variables to say where the mix goes and which microphone to capture. On Linux
.BR cw-mic-mix (1)
does the same with PulseAudio instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-list\-devices ", " \-L
List every readable
.B /dev/input/event*
device, grouped as
.BR unknown ,
.BR keyboard ,
or
.BR paddle .
Groups are ordered so paddles come last, nearest the shell prompt, and the
devices in each group are sorted by event number. A device that reports
the default LEFTCTRL/RIGHTCTRL paddle keys is called out. The category
names are colored only when standard output is a terminal; set
.B NO_COLOR
to disable them. The
.B keyboard
or
.B paddle
guess is a hint only and is not used to select a device.
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Interactively choose the paddle device by tapping the dot lever and then
the dash lever. This is also what a bare
.B cwkeyer
invocation does on an interactive terminal. The device and both key codes
are learned from the taps, so the
.I /dev/input/eventX
name is not needed. If exactly one known paddle adapter is present, the tap
test is skipped and the adapter's table default keys are used instead.
Starts with the default speed, pitch, level, and
weighting, all of which remain adjustable at runtime. A device that looks
like a normal keyboard defaults to
.B nograb
and a paddle defaults to exclusive grab. If both taps use the same key,
.B cwkeyer
offers straight-key mode. Requires an interactive terminal on standard
input.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The side tone settings apply everywhere; the audio output variables are per
platform.
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
Linux only. ALSA PCM to open (default
.BR pulse ).
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_LATENCY_US
Linux only. Requested ALSA latency in microseconds (default 20000). ALSA may negotiate
a different buffer and period; the achieved values are printed at startup.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_LEVEL
Sidetone level as a percentage of full scale, 0\(en100 (default 70). The
level can also be changed while running with
.B 9
and
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_DEBOUNCE_MS
Paddle RC filter time constant in milliseconds, 0\(en50 (default 10). The
8044's recommended 1 M\(*W/0.01 \(*mF network is about 10 ms. Set to 0 to
disable the filter; a shorter value gives a snappier response at high speed.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_WEIGHT_MS
Weighting in milliseconds, \(en25\(en25 (default 0, strict ITU timing). A
constant time is added to every mark and removed from the space that
follows it, so the element period and the character speed are unchanged.
This matches the 8044ABM's RC weight control, which stretches elements by
a fixed amount rather than a fixed ratio, so its relative effect depends on
the keying speed. Can also be changed while running with
.B ,
and
.BR . .
.TP
.B PULSE_SINK
Linux only. PulseAudio/PipeWire sink for the sidetone. If
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
is
.B pulse
and this is unset or empty, it defaults to
.BR cw_out ,
the combined sink created by
.BR cw-mic-mix (1).
.TP
.B CW_MIX_MIC_DEVICE
macOS only, with
.BR mix\-mic .
Input device by name or UID to capture (default: the system input).
Capturing needs the Microphone permission, which macOS attributes to the
program that launched the keyer.
.TP
.B CW_AUDIO_DEVICE
macOS only. Output device by name or UID, which pins it. The default
.RB ( default )
tracks the system output, including later changes, so headphones plugged in
mid-session take the sidetone with them.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, do not color the output of
.B \-\-list\-devices
or the setup wizard.
.SH RUNTIME CONTROLS
If standard input is a readable terminal or pipe,
.B cwkeyer
accepts these single-key controls while it is running:
.TP
.B [
and
.B ]
Slower and faster, 1 WPM per step (limited to 1\(en40 WPM).
.TP
.B -
and
.B =
(or
.BR + )
Lower and higher pitch, 10 Hz per step.
.TP
.B 9
and
.B 0
Quieter and louder, 5 percent per step.
.TP
.B ,
and
.B .
Lighter and heavier weighting, 1 ms per step.
.PP
Pending keys in a pipe are consumed before the keyer treats its closed
writer as end-of-input. Changes take effect on the next element. On a
terminal the current settings are shown on a single status line that is
rewritten in place rather than appended line by line. The controls are read from
standard input, not from the evdev paddle device, so avoid mapping a paddle
to one of these keys.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
stop the keyer and restore the terminal and the input grab.
.B SIGHUP
and
.B SIGQUIT
do the same.
.B SIGTSTP
(Ctrl+Z) releases the terminal and the input device before suspending and
re-establishes them on
.BR SIGCONT .
Ordinary signal cleanup is performed by a dedicated thread, not in an
asynchronous signal handler. Terminal restoration after a fatal crash
cannot be guaranteed; use
.B stty sane
if necessary.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
When the keyer stops it prints a summary to standard error if any ALSA
underruns occurred or if any element started more than a millisecond later
than its nominal mark plus space, together with the worst delay. A run with
no audio problems prints nothing there.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3
12 WPM, 800 Hz on a keyboard.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3 25 700
25 WPM at 700 Hz.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3 25 700 COMMA DOT nograb decode
Remap the paddles, do not grab the device, and decode to text.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/eventX straight
Use the paddles as a straight key instead of an iambic keyer.
.SH FILES
The files below are Linux; on macOS the paddle is a HID device with no node
(see
.BR MACOS ).
.TP
.I /dev/input/eventX
Paddle input device.
.TP
.I /dev/input/
evdev devices; see the README for the recommended udev rule.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evtest (8),
.BR libinput (1),
.BR pactl (1),
.BR cw-mic-mix (1)
and the project README, on Linux.
.PP
On macOS the equivalents are IORegistryExplorer or
.B ioreg (8)
for HID devices, Audio MIDI Setup for audio devices, and the BlackHole driver
for the microphone mix.
